<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Murata Seifū</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1783">1783</a>–<a href="/years/1855">1855</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Murata Seifu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Murata Seifū (1783–1855) was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> official of the Chōshū domain who led its Tenpō-era reforms from 1838. He reduced the domain's heavy debts, cut spending and encouraged and regulated the production of paper, salt and wax, while developing trade through the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimonoseki">Shimonoseki</a>. His reforms strengthened Chōshū's finances ahead of its leading role in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/murata-seifu.jpg" alt="Murata Seifū" width="320" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Murata_Seihu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
